  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "not stated",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper evaluates consistency of weight-space safety regions, but does not test or use the residual-stream difference-in-means refusal direction or any of the listed versions. It explicitly leaves activation-level analysis for future work.",
    "gate_note": "The reported experiments measure cross-dataset overlap of weight-space regions identified by SNIP, Wanda, SafeNeuron, NLSR, and SafeLayer. These are related to safety localization but do not measure the specified residual-stream direction, refusal behavior after its removal or addition, or general capability effects. Results cited from other work are not recorded as rows.",
    "test_reason": "The study measures cross-dataset IoU of weight-space safety regions (SNIP, Wanda, SafeNeuron, NLSR, SafeLayer). It never computes a difference-in-means residual-stream direction, ablates or adds one, or measures refusal after such an intervention. Arditi et al. is cited only in related work and as an over-rejection reference. The authors say activation-level analysis is left for future work. 'cites_only' fits better than 'discusses' because the direction is never argued about.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors find that current weight-space identification methods do not yield stable, dataset-agnostic safety regions, and that utility isolation lowers overlap further. They could not reproduce SafeLayer localization. They frame this as a diagnosis of identification consistency, not a negation of the methods' effectiveness.",
    "recipe": "none",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper identifies and compares weight-space parameter, neuron, layer, and LoRA regions; it does not apply the original residual-stream direction method.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Overlap depends on identification dataset and method; utility-region subtraction generally lowers overlap. In SafeLayer localization, results vary with the over-rejection dataset and its evaluation.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Weight-space safety regions found by SNIP, Wanda, SafeNeuron and NLSR overlap only low to moderately across 10 identification datasets (IoU about 0.01-0.72). Overlap drops further after subtracting the utility region (as low as 0.001). SafeLayer Stage II localization could not be reproduced and was dataset-dependent.",
        "proposed_claim": "Weight-space safety-region identification methods do not yield a stable, dataset-invariant parameter region.",
